Continuous Deployment (CD) Flow – Peer Infrastructure

1. Overview

Peer’s continuous deployment (CD) setup uses a jump host (bastion) to coordinate deployments across multiple servers:

Website / Web app

Backend (PHP)

PostgreSQL database

Monitoring stack

⚙️ The CI (GitHub Actions) environment still uses Docker for testing, linting, and validation —
but the live servers themselves do not run Docker. All deployment scripts act directly on the host OS.

2. Trigger (GitHub Webhook)

Event:
A pull_request event from peer-network/peer-backend (for pre-release, main, or dev branches — to be confirmed) triggers a PHP webhook on the Jump Host.
This webhook decides which server to target first:

either the PostgreSQL server (if SQL files changed), or

the Backend server (if only backend code changed, using the existing DB).

Webhook Actions:

Reads PR data via the GitHub API.

Checks whether .sql files were modified.

✅ If SQL files changed: Connects (via Jump Host) to the PostgreSQL server and runs update-database.sh.

🚫 If no SQL changes: Connects directly to the Backend server to run deploy-backend.sh.

Authentication:
Uses a GitHub token stored securely in .env.deploy.

3. Jump Host

All SSH traffic passes through the Jump Host, which securely connects to:

PostgreSQL Server

Backend Server

Monitoring Server (peer-monitor)

SSH Setup:

Uses ProxyCommand for tunneling.

Authentication key: /home/ubuntu/.ssh/php-db-migr8.

The Jump Host ensures controlled access, traceability, and centralized orchestration of all deployments.

4. Database Deployment Flow (SQL changed)

Script: /home/ubuntu/update-database.sh (on PostgreSQL server)

Steps:

Extract PR number → e.g. peer_db_pr_<PR_NUMBER>.

Clone base database (using createdb --template or pg_dump/psql).

Apply all new .sql migrations sequentially.

On success → notify the Jump Host, which then connects to the Backend Server via SSH to continue deployment.

On failure → abort (backend not triggered).

Rollback logic:
If the backend deployment fails after DB update:

The Jump Host triggers a rollback on the PostgreSQL server.

The PostgreSQL script drops the newly created DB and restores the previous active DB.

Retain the previous DB as active.

Notification:
Discord message via webhook (success/failure + developer mentions).

5. Backend Deployment Flow (Non-Docker For sever)

Script: /home/ubuntu/deploy-backend.sh (on backend server)

Note: the deploy backend.sh script is already set, though there will be changes on it
And here are two scripts:
A one-time setup script, which installs all necessary server dependencies.
A runtime script (shown below), which runs on each deployment when backend code changes.
Steps:

#!/bin/bash
set -e

echo "=== Pulling latest backend code ==="
cd /var/www/html
git pull

echo "=== Updating Composer dependencies ==="
composer install --no-dev --prefer-dist --no-interaction
composer dump-autoload -o

echo "=== Generating backend config files ==="
bash /var/www/html/cd-generate-backend-config.sh

echo "=== Building Rust module if present ==="
if [ -f /var/www/html/tokencalculation/Cargo.toml ]; then
  cd /var/www/html/tokencalculation
  source $HOME/.cargo/env
  cargo build --release
  cd /var/www/html
else
  echo "No Rust module found, skipping build."
fi

echo "=== Fixing ownership and permissions ==="
sudo chown -R www-data:www-data /var/www/html/
sudo find /var/www/html/ -type d -exec chmod 755 {} \;
sudo find /var/www/html/ -type f -exec chmod 644 {} \;

echo "=== Backend runtime update complete ==="

Post-deployment Actions
Restart PHP-FPM and Nginx if required.
Send a Discord notification with deployment status (success/failure).
If deployment fails after an SQL update, the Jump Host triggers a rollback on the PostgreSQL server.

6. Monitoring Layer

Server: peer-monitor

Scripts:

monitor-api.sh / monitor-api-py.sh

Run via cron at regular intervals.

Checks:

Login

List posts 1–20

List posts 21–40 Copy

On failure:
Send Telegram alert with:

Request name

Request + response body

Timestamp

Metrics collected by Prometheus and visualized in Grafana dashboards.

7. Expansion Strategy

Once verified on the single “test” environment:

Replicate same flow to other environments (e.g., production, staging).

Only adjust host IPs, SSH keys, and tokens.

The bastion acts as the central orchestrator across all.


